Option (B) proposes phenol + HCl \(\rightarrow\) chlorobenzene. This is NOT possible.

In phenol the oxygen lone pair is delocalised into the ring, so the \(C-O\) bond acquires partial double-bond character and the carbon is \(sp^{2}\). Nucleophilic attack by \(Cl^{-}\) on an \(sp^{2}\) carbon is very difficult, and \(-OH\) cannot leave as water from a phenol the way it does from an alcohol. Phenols therefore do not undergo the \(-OH \rightarrow -X\) exchange that alcohols undergo with HX.

The others are all real:
(A) anisole + HBr cleaves the ether at the \(sp^{3}\) \(CH_{3}-O\) bond to give phenol + \(CH_{3}Br\);
(C) chlorobenzene + NaOH at high temperature followed by acidification is the Dow process for phenol;
(D) anisole + \(Cl_{2}/AlCl_{3}\) is ordinary electrophilic substitution, para-directed by the activating \(-OCH_{3}\).
